WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.089 --> 00:00:12.530
The term ‘Thalassophobia’ means ‘An
intense fear of deep water.’

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:18.270
It is one of the most primal and understandable
phobias — for the dark abyss has always

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:21.710
been a birthplace of monsters.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:27.289
Lurking below the waves are not only bizarre
species known to science — but a vast multitude

00:00:27.289 --> 00:00:29.960
of imagined leviathans.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.340
Something about the fathomless void floods
the human mind with visions of nightmarish

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:39.570
creatures, which have scared and fascinated
us since we first gazed into the deep.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:46.270
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
dive into depths both real and imagined, and

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.880
discover what terrors lurk within the heart
of the sea...

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.000
“Beginning a descent…

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:00.280
Cruising depth in roughly 40 seconds, standby.”

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:06.690
I’ll begin with the game Iron Lung… where
you play as a convict, sealed inside a cramped

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:11.700
submarine, exploring depths so far below the
vessel’s safety limit that the only window

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.760
has to be welded shut.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:19.450
It’s a good starting point because despite
its retro-style graphics, I believe that,

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:24.570
short of falling into the Mariana Trench,
it’s the closest you can get to experiencing

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.899
the terror of the deep sea firsthand.

00:01:27.899 --> 00:01:33.630
Iron Lung takes place in the wake of an event
called “The Quiet Rapture,” in which every

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:40.320
known star and habitable planet just… vanished,
leaving behind only those who were on space

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:41.320
stations.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:47.140
And one remaining moon is now covered in what
seems to be an ocean of blood — and it’s

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.650
this ocean you’re diving beneath.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.159
Very relaxing stuff.

00:01:52.159 --> 00:01:57.460
With the window welded shut, your only way
of navigating is using a primitive radar system

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:59.899
that pings when you’re near a wall.

00:01:59.899 --> 00:02:04.380
But soon, you start to get pings even when
you’re in open water.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.100
The sole method of checking what’s out there
is using a camera that essentially works like

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:14.370
an old polaroid — taking a few, nail-biting
seconds to process the image.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:20.460
At first, despite the pings on the radar,
you see no monsters… but you do see other

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:21.460
things.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:28.099
Mysterious bones that defy explanation, twisting
structures that almost resemble pipes — even,

00:02:28.099 --> 00:02:32.360
impossibly, what seems to be the remains of
a building.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:36.940
Until eventually you take a picture, and in
the corner…

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:41.140
It's hard to convey how frightening this moment
is.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:45.470
Because of the delay, you know the creature
might be right on top of you, but you take

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:48.409
another picture and it’s… gone.

00:02:48.409 --> 00:02:51.280
Like there was nothing there at all.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.500
As you continue, despite the vastness of the
sea around you, the interior of the sub comes

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:02.310
to feel paradoxically claustrophobic, made
worse by malfunctions like sudden electrical

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:03.310
fires.

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:08.080
Later, the vessel starts flooding, blood rising
up to your waist.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:16.900
Eventually a huge object appears on the radar,
and when you go to take a photo…

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:22.780
This was about where I hit my limit, which
is a testament to how expertly Iron Lung uses

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:26.870
minimalist mechanics to simulate the terror
of the crushing depths.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:32.020
While its ocean of blood is of course fictional,
the dread it conjures seems drawn from real

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:36.530
encounters with the ocean depths…

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:40.190
Frightening experiences with the ocean aren’t
anything new.

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:45.910
Stories of sea monsters, likely inspired by
fleeting encounters with abyssal marine life,

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:48.599
have existed since our first days of seafaring.

00:03:48.599 --> 00:03:54.959
But for much of history, many regarded the
idea of life surviving in the deep as superstition.

00:03:54.959 --> 00:04:00.970
In the 19th century, the popular ‘Abyssus
Theory,’ proposed by naturalist Edward Forbes,

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:07.440
held that no species could not exist deeper
than 300 meters, or 1000 feet below the ocean’s

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:08.440
surface.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.800
While later studies would contradict this
idea, it wasn’t until 1930 that someone

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.350
attempted to dive below such depths to see
for themselves.

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:22.730
Naturalist William Beebe, alongside Otis Barton
plunged further into the abyss than any human

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:26.610
had before in a metal tank called the Bathysphere.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.920
And what they saw changed science forever.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:35.310
Fish that flashed with bioluminescent color,
see-through predators of the deep, surreal

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:39.160
invertebrates that glided on fantastical fins.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:44.039
One can only imagine what it was like for
someone in the early 20th century to see the

00:04:44.039 --> 00:04:47.260
alien sights of the deep for the first time.

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:51.580
Especially when you consider that the men
were observing the abyss in a heavily altered

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:56.580
state of mind, as both were battered and concussed
from a bumpy voyage down.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:01.449
Perhaps it’s no surprise, then, that not
all the fish they claimed to have seen have

00:05:01.449 --> 00:05:03.050
been identified.

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:08.220
Beebe stated he saw a bizarre creature he
named the Five-Lined Constellation Fish, and

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:13.670
while no such species has ever been spotted
again, the description bears a striking resemblance

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:18.580
to a deep-sea comb jelly, which Beebe’s
might have interpreted as a fish — unable

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:21.280
to comprehend its true form.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:26.479
Even more incredibly, Beebe claimed that two
giant dragonfish, larger than the sub itself

00:05:26.479 --> 00:05:30.300
swam by — which have also never been documented
since.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:35.730
It’s possible that once again Beebe saw
another deep-sea species and misidentified

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:41.569
it… or it’s possible these giants have
simply eluded detection…

00:05:41.569 --> 00:05:46.500
In either case, the Bathysphere’s descent
and others like it flooded the public consciousness

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:48.910
with visions of the watery depths.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:54.910
Over the next few decades, numerous leviathans
rose up from the abyss and into early cinema

00:05:54.910 --> 00:06:01.229
— with movies about aquatic monsters terrorizing
the land becoming exceedingly popular.

00:06:01.229 --> 00:06:06.919
One of the most famous of these films was
the 1954 adaption of Jules Verne’s 20,000

00:06:06.919 --> 00:06:11.740
Leagues Under the Sea — a movie with origins
even older than the Bathysphere.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:17.540
The novel it’s based on comes from 1870,
and actually predicted the invention of submarines

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:22.919
— making it foundational to all sci-fi interpretations
of the deep sea.

00:06:22.919 --> 00:06:27.479
Leading into the modern era, the vast majority
of films about the ocean have continued to

00:06:27.479 --> 00:06:30.009
portray it as a hostile realm.

00:06:30.009 --> 00:06:34.850
Nearly everything about the uncharted depths
lends itself to horror — from the crushing

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:40.160
pressure, to the low visibility, to the bizarre
creatures that swim in the gloom.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:45.220
The deep sea can be so alien, some works of
sci-fi have imagined it as home to literal

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:46.390
aliens.

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:51.849
In James Cameron’s The Abyss, humans make
first contact with a group of advanced aquatic

00:06:51.849 --> 00:06:55.849
extraterrestrials who settled in Earth’s
oceans long ago.

00:06:55.849 --> 00:07:00.970
The film seems to take inspiration from “In
the Abyss” by early sci-fi novelist H.G.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:06.979
Wells — in which an explorer discovers a
similar hidden society of intelligent, undersea

00:07:06.979 --> 00:07:09.250
beings.

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:14.250
Perhaps it should be no surprise the ocean
is portrayed as a hideaway for extra-terrestrials,

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:19.690
as many real deep-sea species seem like they’d
be right at home on another planet.

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:24.900
Take, for example, the Barreleye Fish — an
animal with translucent skin that allows it

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:27.860
to look upwards through its own head to spot
prey.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:34.010
Or the Vampire Squid, a cephalopod whose underside
is covered with fearsome-looking spikes to

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:35.750
ward off predators.

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:41.520
The lifeforms of the depths can be so unsettling,
it’s understandable that most media interprets

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.360
it as a terrifying realm...

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:51.030
Yet a greater understanding of the oceans
hasn’t just led to fearful artistic reactions.

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:56.410
Abzû (Ab-zoo) is a soothing video game that
makes a stirring case for the beauty of the

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:57.800
undersea realm.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:02.430
Following the voyage of a diver through stunning
marine environments, the gameplay involves

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:05.099
no combat or survival mechanics.

00:08:05.099 --> 00:08:10.630
Instead, you spend your time drifting through
shoals of simulated fish, taking the flipper

00:08:10.630 --> 00:08:15.690
of a porpoise and leaping over the waves,
or diving with giants of the deep.

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:20.819
Abzú invites you into the beating heart of
the ocean, where every moment thrums with

00:08:20.819 --> 00:08:22.630
the rhythm of life.

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:27.960
At the same time, it is a game that politely
asks you to let your mind wander, to let go

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:32.750
of your fears and meditate on the calm vistas
around you.

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:38.890
But even in the cheeriest, most brightly-lit
renderings of the ocean, there are often moments

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:41.760
where the dread of the vast abyss creeps in.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:47.640
One major example of this appears in Beyond
Blue, an underwater exploration game where

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:50.779
you catalogue different species of marine
life.

00:08:50.779 --> 00:08:56.040
Compared to Abzú, Beyond Blue takes a less
stylized but equally peaceful approach to

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:58.480
underwater exploration.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:03.279
Yet even though it’s not intended to frighten,
there are parts of this game I find scarier

00:09:03.279 --> 00:09:05.940
than the entirety of certain horror titles.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:11.370
There’s a moment in Beyond Blue where from
the pitch-black void, a giant squid attacks

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:15.610
your camera, before disappearing again into
the unknown.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:23.880
The game suggests that even in an educational
context, the deep can be unintentionally unsettling.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:27.529
Perhaps the ultimate example of accidental
horror is Subnautica.

00:09:27.529 --> 00:09:33.579
A series that plunges you into the alien seas
of a distant planet, Subnautica and its sequel

00:09:33.579 --> 00:09:38.649
bring you face to face with so many nightmarish
aquatic predators they’re often described

00:09:38.649 --> 00:09:41.570
as horror games — or at least horror adjacent.

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:45.670
Yet that wasn’t the intention when the game
was in initial development.

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:50.920
Originally, Subnautica was planned to be a
more relaxed cruise through an aquatic realm,

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:55.520
but early testers kept getting frightened
by the unknown depths — leading the developers

00:09:55.520 --> 00:10:00.990
to realize the potential the game had as a
more intense experience.

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:05.050
Yet the power of Subnautica comes from more
than just jumpscares.

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:09.950
The game will stun you with the vastness of
megafaunal filter feeders, befuddle you with

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:16.440
the bizarreness of unclassifiable oddities,
bewilder you with the blurred light of bioluminescent

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:17.440
wonders.

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:22.540
Though not set on Earth, more than perhaps
any game, Subnautica presents the experience

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:27.790
of the ocean in its totality — a realm that
inspires both awe and terror.

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:33.310
I think it’s curious, then, that despite
crafting a painstakingly complex, diverse

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:38.850
biosphere of simulated life, Subnautica is
typically remembered as a game designed only

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:39.850
to terrify.

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:44.920
In today’s market, Subnautica doesn’t
seem like it should be able to frighten audiences.

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:50.170
There’s no killer chasing you, no axe wielding
maniac just around the corner — threats

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:54.700
that are much closer to us land-dwellers than
something thousands of feet below the waves.

00:10:54.700 --> 00:11:00.070
And yet the dread that Subnautica evokes can
be overwhelming, all-encompassing, to the

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:04.551
point where you forget the more peaceful parts
of the ocean and vow to never dip a toe in

00:11:04.551 --> 00:11:07.670
the water again.

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:12.620
Even when the ocean isn’t presented as something
frightening, the vast majority of humans still

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:16.360
feel some uneasiness towards immeasurable
deep.

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:21.790
In regions where the light dims and visibility
drops off, our rational mind fades out and

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:27.040
our animal mind checks in and gets to work
frightening us — conjuring images of immense

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:31.860
leviathans twisting just out of sight…

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:36.470
Of course, it doesn’t help that there are
leviathans in the depths.

00:11:36.470 --> 00:11:41.640
Abyssal Gigantism is a scientific term for
the tendency of deep-sea dwelling animals

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:45.330
to grow far larger than their shallow-water
relatives.

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:50.200
While there are some proposed explanations
for this phenomenon, the inaccessibility of

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:54.769
abyssal habitats means that there’s still
much we don’t know about the giants of our

00:11:54.769 --> 00:11:55.769
own oceans.

00:11:55.769 --> 00:11:59.600
It can make you feel like anything might be
down there...

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:01.170
And that can cause problems.

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:06.889
A few years ago, an infamous Discovery Channel
documentary took advantage of the ocean’s

00:12:06.889 --> 00:12:12.980
potential to conceal monsters and suggested
the long extinct shark, the Megalodon, still

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:14.360
swam in the oceans.

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:18.620
This concept gained so much traction in the
public eye that it seemingly inspired The

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:24.250
Meg – a movie in which a somehow-still living
Megalodon attacks modern beachgoers.

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:28.250
While it’s easy to laugh at the fact that
so many people believed a prehistoric shark

00:12:28.250 --> 00:12:34.560
still lived that a major studio made a multi-million-dollar
blockbuster about it, this cultural phenomenon

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:40.949
shows the power of the ocean’s unknowable
vastness to trigger human imagination.

00:12:40.949 --> 00:12:45.770
Yet many of us had our trust shattered years
earlier, with the release of a mocumentary

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:48.490
called Mermaids: The Body Found.

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:53.639
This widely aired faux documentary sets out
to convince you that Mermaids are real hominids

00:12:53.639 --> 00:12:59.100
descended from aquatic apes — and for the
time, it was pretty convincing: using fake

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:05.380
cave paintings, manufactured artifacts, and
even full CGI recreations to lend plausibility

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:07.270
to the absurd concept.

00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:12.649
The film honestly goes so far into fictional
mermaid biology it almost feels like a work

00:13:12.649 --> 00:13:14.990
of Speculative Evolution.

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:20.300
It even folds in real-world unusual phenomena,
like The Bloop, and claims they were the result

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:21.630
of mermaids.

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:26.779
For those of you who don’t know, The Bloop
is a strange underwater noise recorded in

00:13:26.779 --> 00:13:31.970
2002 that was many orders of magnitude louder
than even the largest whale calls.

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:36.430
While the sound instigated speculation of
an undiscovered creature, further analysis

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:40.260
shows it was almost certainly the result of
a glacier moving.

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:46.160
But the speed at which these hoaxes and misunderstanding
have spread shows that the skeptical part

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:49.949
of our psyches shut down when gazing into
the abyss.

00:13:49.949 --> 00:13:55.110
It’s no accident that one of the most widely
reported childhood fears is of a shark lurking

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:58.130
in the deep end of a swimming pool.

00:13:58.130 --> 00:14:02.360
Plausibility doesn’t matter — when it
comes to deep water, anything could be waiting

00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:05.990
in the darkness...

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:11.579
Paradoxically, sometimes the best way to convey
the power of the ocean is to reveal very little

00:14:11.579 --> 00:14:12.579
of it.

00:14:12.579 --> 00:14:18.260
And few pieces of media make use of the unseen
better than the anxiety inducing Water Womb

00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:19.260
World.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:25.110
A deeply surreal pixel art game created by
indie designer Yames, the game follows a religious

00:14:25.110 --> 00:14:29.940
man who believes he’s found the origin point
of life deep below the waves.

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:33.509
The game is dark — not just thematically,
but literally.

00:14:33.509 --> 00:14:39.080
At any given moment, the vast majority of
pixels on screen are pure black as you gaze

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:43.250
out from your helmet into the unyielding vacuum
of the ocean.

00:14:43.250 --> 00:14:47.340
Much of the gameplay involves sifting through
silt, where you find things that should be

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:52.079
there like a pair of goggles… and things
that definitely shouldn’t, like a human

00:14:52.079 --> 00:14:55.149
skull that’s over ten thousand years old.

00:14:55.149 --> 00:14:59.959
The more unsettling objects you find, the
more you become convinced that a big scare

00:14:59.959 --> 00:15:05.870
is coming, that any moment now something will
pop out from the darkness, but nothing comes,

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:16.399
until back at your undersea lab, you hear
this… (eerie call).

00:15:16.399 --> 00:15:21.360
Returning to the abyss, you find the darkness
is more all-encompassing than ever… and

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:25.149
then, from the gloom, emerges this.

00:15:25.149 --> 00:15:30.870
…The main character interprets the being
as an angel, and continues to dig up increasingly

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:35.230
disturbing artifacts, while their mind and
body become increasingly diseased.

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:41.451
And the ending comes not long after that — for
Water Womb World is a short game, only offering

00:15:41.451 --> 00:15:48.190
a tiny glimpse of a far wider realm of nightmares…
which only adds to how unsettling it is.

00:15:48.190 --> 00:15:52.850
In the same way that the ocean’s dreadful
power comes from how much of it we’ve never

00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:59.980
seen, the game’s restraint makes the atmosphere
of unease all the more palpable.

00:15:59.980 --> 00:16:04.279
Another indie game that captures the ocean’s
might despite never showing much of it is

00:16:04.279 --> 00:16:06.140
In Other Waters.

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:11.540
Although the game is focused on exploring
a vast alien sea teaming with life, the entirety

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:17.000
of the game’s environment is communicated
through nothing but text and minimal icons.

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:21.250
In most games, you have a heads up display
in the bottom corner to help you keep track

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:23.149
of various in-game systems.

00:16:23.149 --> 00:16:28.100
But when playing In Other Waters, the entire
game is the heads-up display.

00:16:28.100 --> 00:16:32.830
While this can take some getting used to,
it’s a clever way for a smaller budget game

00:16:32.830 --> 00:16:34.700
to feel expansive.

00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:40.190
As you play, you learn to visualize each lifeform
as they’re described — picturing luminous

00:16:40.190 --> 00:16:45.970
creatures gliding through the water, armored
lifeforms with tough shells, or fungal organisms

00:16:45.970 --> 00:16:47.990
that grow in endless rows.

00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:53.230
It’s a game that embodies the enigmatic
nature of the ocean — taking advantage of

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:59.750
the human instinct to imagine what swims below
opaque waters.

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:05.730
The ocean doesn’t need to be filled with
imagined monsters to instill dread, though.

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:08.500
Even singular, real datapoints can be haunting.

00:17:08.500 --> 00:17:15.000
Point Nemo, for example, is a spot in the
Pacific that seems utterly uninteresting — except

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:18.910
for the fact that it’s the place on earth
furthest from any land.

00:17:18.910 --> 00:17:23.880
Should you find yourself floating at Point
Nemo, the closest people to you are the astronauts

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:28.640
aboard the International Space Station when
it passes overhead.

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:34.200
Yet monsters are a primal part of our relationship
with the deep, and for our last stop on this

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:39.360
plunge into the void — there’s one final
leviathan we need to confront.

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:47.150
SCP-3000 is an entry into the famous community-driven
paranormal anthology the SCP series, and is

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:51.780
perhaps the ultimate personification of the
sea monster archetype.

00:17:51.780 --> 00:17:59.640
SCP-3000 is, in the simplest terms, a giant
Eel, measuring over eight hundred and fifty

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:05.650
kilometers, or five hundred miles in length,
located somewhere in the Indian Ocean.

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:11.030
It also secretes a substance that causes amnesia
and mental shutdown — although just seeing

00:18:11.030 --> 00:18:14.710
such a large creature could cause anyone to
lose their mind.

00:18:14.710 --> 00:18:18.280
And that’s… basically the whole concept.

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:23.470
Considering how complex many entries into
the SCP series are, it’s noteworthy that

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:30.409
SCP-3000 is one of the most famous — inspiring
incredible fan art and animation despite its

00:18:30.409 --> 00:18:31.809
relative simplicity.

00:18:31.809 --> 00:18:37.289
There’s something about the purity of an
unfathomably vast eel that just works — made

00:18:37.289 --> 00:18:42.299
all the more intriguing by the fact that the
eel isn’t aggressive, or curious.

00:18:42.299 --> 00:18:44.120
It simply is.

00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:49.549
To me, it’s the ultimate, oddly reassuring
proof that even in the age of the internet,

00:18:49.549 --> 00:18:56.430
our innate fear and fascination with giants
below the waves hasn’t left us.

00:18:56.430 --> 00:19:00.760
In the end, perhaps it shouldn’t come as
a surprise that the watery abyss still holds

00:19:00.760 --> 00:19:03.230
power over the human psyche.

00:19:03.230 --> 00:19:08.890
The ocean is deep space in our back yard — an
alien realm of alien creatures right here

00:19:08.890 --> 00:19:09.909
on Earth.

00:19:09.909 --> 00:19:14.500
As long as a significant percentage of the
deep blue remains unmapped, we will never

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:18.590
cease to speculate on what swims beneath the
waves.

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:23.510
Despite our rational minds telling us otherwise,
in the depths where light cannot penetrate,

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:30.919
the monsters of our oldest myths will continue
to lurk — forever just out of sight.

00:19:30.919 --> 00:19:32.700
As always, thanks for watching.

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:38.179
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:19:38.179 --> 00:19:42.650
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:19:42.650 --> 00:19:51.780
See you in the next video.

